    {
      "id": "TE-C65-welcome",
      "sequence": 2080,
      "title": "స్వాగతం (svāgataṁ) — a welcome",
      "headword": "స్వాగతం",
      "romanization": "svāgataṁ",
      "gloss": "a welcome",
      "script": "telugu",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:fed2c9bad28164d6",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did iṅkā mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: స్వాగతం",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "స్వాగతం (svāgataṁ) — \"a welcome\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Keep this well apart from పరవాలేదు. పరవాలేదు is what you say after somebody thanks you. స్వాగతం (svāgataṁ) is what you say when they walk in. English hands the one word \"welcome\" both jobs; Telugu does not."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is Sanskrit, and it comes apart neatly: su- \"well\" and āgata \"come\" — \"well come\", which is what the English word is too, arrived by an entirely separate road."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A house says it with the door open and a gauravaṁ behind it. The ముగ్గు at the step is the same sentence without any words in it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first thing said at a door."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "svāgataṁ",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *svāgataṁ*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "it once more, slowly",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: it once more, slowly]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "svāgataṁ, then paravālēdu, and say which one answers a thank-you",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *svāgataṁ*, then *paravālēdu*, and say which one answers a thank-you]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does స్వాగతం (svāgataṁ) mean? (\"a welcome\".) Say it once more."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "TE-C65-manners",
      "sequence": 2090,
      "title": "మర్యాద (maryāda) — good manners",
      "headword": "మర్యాద",
      "romanization": "maryāda",
      "gloss": "good manners",
      "script": "telugu",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:17254728f23d3ab4",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did svāgataṁ mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: మర్యాద",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "మర్యాద (maryāda) — \"good manners\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "మర్యాద (maryāda) is the doing half of gauravaṁ. Respect is what you feel for somebody; మర్యాద (maryāda) is what you do about it — standing up, offering the కుర్చీ, serving the atithi before yourself."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The word is Sanskrit and its first sense was a boundary line. Manners as a line you do not step over is a picture worth keeping hold of."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "మర్యాదగా is how it usually reaches a person: మర్యాదగా మాట్లాడు, \"speak with manners\", which sounds sterner in English than it does in Telugu. It is closer to \"be decent about it\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two: what you feel and what you do."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "maryāda",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *maryāda*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "it once more, slowly",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: it once more, slowly]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "maryāda, then gauravaṁ, and say which one somebody can watch you doing",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *maryāda*, then *gauravaṁ*, and say which one somebody can watch you doing]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does మర్యాద (maryāda) mean? (\"good manners\".) Say it once more."
            }
          ]
        }
