    {
      "id": "TE-C65-trust",
      "sequence": 2120,
      "title": "నమ్మకం (nammakaṁ) — trust",
      "headword": "నమ్మకం",
      "romanization": "nammakaṁ",
      "gloss": "trust",
      "script": "telugu",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:712e5e6e134c55ab",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did anumati mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: నమ్మకం",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "నమ్మకం (nammakaṁ) — \"trust\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "నమ్మకం (nammakaṁ) is trust, and it is the one word here that is Telugu's own. It is built on నమ్ము, \"to believe\", with the ending that turns an action into the thing itself."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The four before it are Sanskrit and they are all about form: a welcome, manners, a request, permission. నమ్మకం (nammakaṁ) is what the form is for. A house that does all four and is still not trusted has been doing something else."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The word also carries \"belief\" in the religious sense, and Telugu is content to let one word hold both. Trusting a person and believing a thing are the same act pointed in two directions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five: స్వాగతం (svāgataṁ), మర్యాద (maryāda), మనవి (manavi), అనుమతి (anumati), నమ్మకం (nammakaṁ). Enough to bring somebody in, ask something of them, and say ధన్యవాదములు when they agree."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "nammakaṁ",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *nammakaṁ*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "it once more, slowly",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: it once more, slowly]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "all five in order, then say dhanyavādamulu to whoever gave you the last one",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: all five in order, then say *dhanyavādamulu* to whoever gave you the last one]"
